Documentation of Xvid
=====================

INSTALL
-------

This file describes the steps to build and install Xvid on supported
platforms.

NOTE FOR MacOS X PLATFORM
-------------------------

Many versions of yasm/nasm are known to be buggy for Mach-O output.
Whereas nasm seems to be less reliable than yasm. Therefore, yasm
is highly recommended for Mac OS X target.

* yasm 0.7.99.2161 or later is required for Mac OS X build. *

http://www.tortall.net/projects/yasm/

VARIABLE FRAMERATE ENCODING
===========================

The two pass rate controller can follow the real duration of every frame
instead of a single constant frame rate. This is useful for material that
mixes normal motion with long static holds.

Encoder parameters
------------------

Set `xvid_enc_create_t.fincr` to zero to select variable framerate mode. In
that mode `fbase` must be in [1..65535] and is the shared time base: the
duration of every input frame is `fincr/fbase` seconds, where the per-frame
fincr is passed in `xvid_enc_frame_t.fincr`. Every input frame must carry a
positive fincr; the encoder rejects frames without one. Do not duplicate a
frame to emulate a hold, give that one frame its long duration instead.

The variable framerate path currently supports the simple encoding mode
only: one thread, `max_bframes` equal to zero and `frame_drop_ratio` equal
to zero. Other combinations are rejected at creation time. Fixed framerate
encoding and the existing B frame usage keep their historical behaviour.

Timestamp handling
------------------

VOP timestamps are cumulative: frame i starts at the sum of the durations of
frames 0..i-1 expressed in the fbase time base. The cumulative arithmetic is
64 bit, so legal schedules longer than 32 bits are handled.

First pass statistics file
--------------------------

When the first pass runs with `fincr == 0`, the statistics file starts with
a timing header:

    # XVID_TIMING_V1 <fbase>

and every statistics line is preceded by one duration line:

    # XVID_DURATION <frame_num> <fincr>

`frame_num` starts at zero and increments by one for every statistics line.
The seven historical columns (and the optional eighth `scaled_length`
column) keep their meaning. Fixed framerate first pass files do not contain
either extension, so existing files remain readable exactly as before.

Second pass
-----------

The complete timing schedule is read and validated when the plugin is
created, never inferred from an average frame rate:

- A timed file used with a variable framerate second pass (`fincr == 0`)
  must use the same time base as the encoder.
- A timed file used with a fixed framerate second pass is rejected, and a
  variable framerate second pass rejects a file without a timing header.
- Every statistics line must have exactly one duration, with consecutive
  frame numbers, positive durations and a total that cannot overflow the
  subsequent arithmetic. Missing, duplicated, zero, negative, out of range
  or mismatched schedules make creation fail.
- At encode time each frame's fincr must match the duration recorded for
  that frame; otherwise the encode aborts with an error.

For a positive bitrate the byte target is

    floor(bitrate * sum(fincr) / (8 * fbase))

using the real total duration even when it is shorter than one second,
followed by the historical `container_frame_overhead` subtraction. A
negative bitrate still means a target file size in KiB.

VBV and curve scaling
---------------------

Frame i occupies the half open interval [T(i), T(i+1)) with T accumulated
from zero in seconds. Before consuming a frame's bytes the buffer is
refilled with `vbv_maxrate * duration / 8` bytes, capped at `vbv_size`, and
then charged the planned frame demand; a negative fill is an underflow.

The peak rate is checked at each frame end over the complete frames whose
end time falls in `(T_end - 1, T_end]`; a frame ending exactly one second
before is not part of the window. The scene minimum interval and the
R(k,n) term in the scaler use the same elapsed-time model, so the
historical one second scene interval stays one second and R is the bytes
that actually enter the buffer over that span. The 0.9 safety factor and
the 10% low water mark are retained, and the scaled plan is re-checked with
the same time model.

Only the two pass *plan* is constrained this way; the actual encode keeps
the existing quantizer error feedback.

Introspection
-------------

`xvid_plugin_2pass2` accepts `XVID_PLG_2PASS2_QUERY` with a
`xvid_plugin_2pass2_query_t` after creation. It reports the time model, the
exact target and the VBV outcome, and optionally fills the planned per-frame
sizes. It is read-only and intended for diagnostics and tests.

Examples and checks
-------------------

`xvidcore/examples/xvid_vfr_2pass.c` builds a short clip that alternates
moving frames with long holds, runs both passes and saves the first pass
file and the elementary stream.

`tests/vfr_2pass.c` covers the timing, budget, VBV and compatibility cases.
It can be built with a C compiler against the library; `sh devcheck/baseline.sh`
checks the unchanged fixed framerate two-pass path and
`sh devcheck/target.sh` builds and runs the variable framerate test and
example.

